          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How do I know whether a three-character category can stand on its own as a usable code?",
          "answer": "An ICD-10-CM code can be between three and seven characters long. The first three characters represent the code category. If no further characters follow, the three-character category can be used as a standalone code, and no decimal point is used.\n\nHowever, if a code requires further characters to add detail—such as cause, anatomical site, severity, or laterality—a decimal point will follow the first three characters. For example, in the code E11.9, the \"E11\" is the category, and the \".9\" adds more specific detail. A seventh character, if present, typically describes information about the encounter itself rather than the disease.\n\nThis information comes from the document \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection.\"\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
